  4. REGISTER 1404 -- four pairs whose corridors meet at a single point
     Mo  (-0.0000, 1.0000)      and Tc  (1.0000, 4.2361)       meet at 1.000000   a single point
     Gd  (0.7071, 1.7071)       and Tb  (-inf, 0.7071)         meet at 0.707107   a single point
     Cm  (1.3660, 1.9841)       and Bk  (-0.0000, 1.3660)      meet at 1.366025   a single point
     Lr  (1.9841, 2.4409)       and Rf  (0.5774, 1.9841)       meet at 1.984059   a single point
     Empty as an open interval, so the second reset is forced and the two are
     one event.  This is the derived form of what walkresets.py measures as a
     boundary touch: `a` does not move, the strict test simply fails.

  5. REGISTER 1402 -- the count belongs to the policy, not to the corridor
     1402 prints: nearest 10 · always-lower 12 · always-upper 17 · midpoint 21
                  · farther 22 · 200 random interior, 18 to 33, median 26
     POLICY-NOT-DEFINED: 1402 does not define its policies precisely enough to
     reproduce those five counts, and the random figure needs its seed. What
     reproduces is the CLAIM, which is that the count varies widely:
       policy                  closed test   open test
       nearest endpoint                  9          61
       always the lower                 10          69
       always the upper                 10          69
       the midpoint                     20          21
       the farther endpoint             12          75
     9 to 75 recalibrations over the same 106 steps and the same corridors.
     So membership cannot be the derived condition and emptiness can, which is
     1402's point and is confirmed.

  WHAT THIS ANSWERS
     Section 34.6 gives no reason for its reset claims. The corpus has one, it is
     exact, and it is about intervals rather than subshells. Nothing is repaired.
